.TH FALCHION-LIGHT 1 "2026-10-15" "Falchion Light" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
falchion-light \- control wired ROG Falchion RX Low Profile lighting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B falchion-light
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B falchion-light info
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B falchion-light probe
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B falchion-light set
.I mode color
.RB [ \-\-brightness " 0..100" ]
.RB [ \-\-speed " slow|medium|fast" ]
.RB [ \-\-save ]
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B falchion-light
controls lighting through the existing Linux hidraw driver on a wired ASUS
ROG Falchion RX Low Profile keyboard, USB ID 0b05:1b04, interface 01.
It requires Python 3 and device access granted by the installed udev rule.
No daemon or custom kernel module is needed.
.PP
Commands can be run from any directory. Each
.B set
command specifies a complete effect, color, and brightness;
previous settings are not read back or preserved automatically.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
Identify the wired lighting interface and verify its descriptor.
Does not open the device or send HID commands.
.TP
.B probe
Verify the opened device and send a version query.
Print the device identification and raw response without changing lighting.
.TP
.BI "set " "mode color"
Query the device, then send the requested lighting effect.
See MODES and OPTIONS below.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B static
Steady light in the specified color.
.TP
.B breathing
Fade the specified color in and out.
.TP
.B cycle
Cycle through rainbow colors. A color argument is still required,
but its value is ignored by this effect; use
.BR 000000 .
.TP
.B wave
Move a rainbow wave across the keys, with different colors visible at once.
A color argument is required but ignored; use
.BR 000000 .
Uses three lighting packets and runs in the keyboard hardware.
.TP
.B reactive
Light keys in response to keypresses using the specified color.
.TP
.B starry
Starry-night effect using the specified color.
.TP
.B raindrop
Raindrop effect using the specified color.
.SH ARGUMENTS AND OPTIONS
.TP
.I color
Exactly six hexadecimal RGB digits, such as
.BR 336699 .
Uppercase and lowercase digits are accepted.
An optional leading # must be quoted in the shell, for example
.BR \(aq#336699\(aq .
Named colors such as blue or red are not accepted.
.TP
.BI "\-\-brightness " number
Integer from 0 (off) to 100 (maximum). Default: 50.
Repeat a command with nonzero brightness to restore lighting.
.TP
.BI "\-\-speed " speed
Accepts
.BR slow ,
.BR medium ,
or
.BR fast .
Default: medium. Controls animation speed; static lighting does not animate.
.TP
.B \-\-save
Send the community-documented apply/save command after setting the effect.
The keyboard acknowledged saving rainbow wave at slow speed and 50 percent
brightness; persistence after a power cycle still needs confirmation.
Without it, no explicit save command is sent.
Persistence across power cycles is unverified.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Print the proposed output report and exit without discovering or opening
hardware. Does not send a version query, effect command, or save command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit. Available at the top level and after each command.
.SH EXAMPLES
Identify the device:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light info
    falchion-light probe
.fi
.PP
Steady blue at half brightness:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set static 336699 \-\-brightness 50
.fi
.PP
Warm orange at 25 percent brightness:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set static ff8800 \-\-brightness 25
.fi
.PP
Blue breathing:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set breathing 336699 \-\-speed slow \-\-brightness 50
.fi
.PP
Rainbow color cycle:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set cycle 000000 \-\-speed medium \-\-brightness 50
.fi
.PP
Rainbow wave moving across the keys:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set wave 000000 \-\-speed slow \-\-brightness 50
.fi
.PP
Blue on keypress, starry night, and raindrops:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set reactive 336699 \-\-brightness 50
    falchion-light set starry 336699 \-\-speed slow \-\-brightness 50
    falchion-light set raindrop 336699 \-\-speed medium \-\-brightness 50
.fi
.PP
Turn lighting off, then restore steady blue:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set static 336699 \-\-brightness 0
    falchion-light set static 336699 \-\-brightness 50
.fi
.PP
Inspect a command without accessing hardware:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set breathing 336699 \-\-speed slow \-\-dry-run
.fi
.PP
Save rainbow wave to the keyboard (power-cycle persistence unverified):
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set wave 000000 \-\-speed slow \-\-brightness 50 \-\-save
.fi
.PP
Show command-specific help:
.PP
.nf
    falchion-light set \-\-help
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Command completed, or help was displayed.
A successful lighting response should still be checked visually.
.TP
.B 1
Device discovery, validation, access, or communication failed.
If a response times out after a write, the lighting outcome is unconfirmed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/falchion-light
Installed executable.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/man/man1/falchion-light.1
Installed manual page.
.TP
.I /etc/udev/rules.d/70-falchion-light.rules
Grants the active local desktop user access to the wired lighting interface.
.TP
.I README.md (in the source checkout)
Source documentation, validation notes, and removal instructions.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Only wired USB is supported. Receiver and Bluetooth control are unsupported.
As of 2026-10-15, static blue at 50 percent brightness was visually confirmed
on this unit. Other animated effects besides the confirmed rainbow wave
follow community protocol evidence and remain unverified here.
.PP
The wave command returned expected acknowledgments for all three packets;
The rainbow wave was visually confirmed on the test keyboard.
.PP
The program requires exactly one matching device and an exact supported
descriptor. It does not update firmware, reset the keyboard, accept arbitrary
HID commands, or read typing interfaces.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
If no matching device is found, connect the keyboard with a USB data cable
and select wired mode.
If access is denied, verify the installed udev rule and reconnect the keyboard
while logged into the local desktop session.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR udev (7)
